  "title": "Ukraine Says It Sank a Russian Warship in Libya. Why Was It in Benghazi?",
  "deck": "The Russian corvette stopped to refuel and resupply under a cooperation agreement, eastern Libyan authorities said. The strike puts a spotlight on Moscow’s military ties in the Mediterranean.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "news",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T12:05:21Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-23T12:05:21Z",
  "body": [
    "A Russian warship stopped at a Libyan naval base to take on fuel and supplies. By the next day, Ukraine was claiming it had sunk the ship about 1,500 miles from the front line.",
    "Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy said Monday that Ukrainian sea drones struck the Russian corvette Merkury on Sunday at Benghazi, on Libya’s Mediterranean coast. Eastern Libya’s government said the ship was struck while docked at the naval base. Two Libyan officials told the Associated Press the corvette was severely damaged, and satellite imagery published by CBS News showed it capsized at the port. The Kremlin declined to comment when asked about the attack.",
    "What Russia was doing in Benghazi",
    "Eastern Libyan authorities said the Merkury stopped to refuel and resupply on its way back to Russia under a cooperation agreement. That detail gives the strike a significance beyond the loss Kyiv is claiming. Benghazi was serving a practical purpose for the Russian navy, giving it a place to replenish a ship during a Mediterranean voyage.",
    "Ukraine’s security service said the Merkury regularly escorted Russian naval convoys. Zelenskyy said Ukraine also struck the cargo ship MV Angara off Libya on Sunday and described it as a vessel Russia used to transport weapons. Those claims come from Kyiv. The confirmed overlap in the accounts concerns the strike on the corvette at Benghazi.",
    "The fallout reaches Libya",
    "The attack also raised a question inside Libya about whether its territory had been used to prepare the operation. Authorities in the east asked the United Nations to investigate possible involvement from western Libya, according to AP. Their request did not establish where the operation was prepared or whether western Libyan authorities had any role.",
    "That distinction matters for a country with rival authorities. Eastern Libya’s government has acknowledged the Russian resupply arrangement. Its allegation about preparations in western Libya calls for separate evidence.",
    "What one strike can tell us",
    "Much of Ukraine’s naval campaign has played out in the Black Sea. The Benghazi strike shows that at least one port servicing Russian warships in the Mediterranean has already been caught up in the conflict.",
    "The next development to watch is whether Russian naval vessels keep calling at Benghazi. Continued visits would show that Moscow and its eastern Libyan partners are willing to maintain the arrangement after the attack. Any confirmed change in where Russian ships refuel or resupply would help show whether the strike had consequences beyond the Merkury itself."
  ],
